ble pension contribution can be done if he's still working – none.' The adviser notes indicate this was not confirmed at time of fact find","Report objective 6 (do not move Chartered savings): Extraction summary notes this appears in the canonical objectives list but extraction ambiguity states 'OBJ6 should be classified under constraints rather than objectives' and 'appears as a fact in the Your current situation section rather than as a stated objective'. Not listed as an objective in [P3] Fact Find objectives section"],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report asserts six client objectives with priority ranking 1–6, all marked as client-stated. The evidence base allows partial corroboration but reveals material inconsistencies and gaps. Objective 1 (Risk Level 4 alignment) is supported by the agreed profile evident in [P12], but the Risk Profile Report [P10] shows conflicting records: a table cites 'Agreed risk level 5' while the contents page shows 'Risk profile 4', and [P18] records no discussion notes on how the risk level was agreed—a control gap given that risk is fundamental to suitability. Objective 2 (tax-efficient investment and ISA transfer) is partially supported: [P3] confirms the inheritance investment need and the £2,200 target; however, the ISA transfer is recorded in [P3] as conditional ('would have to be really worth his while - not dead set on doing this') and in [P15] as unlikely ('Possibly move ISAs – not likely though'). The report presents this as a primary objective without qualification; the evidence shows client reluctance. Objective 3 (retirement income target of £2,200 net per month) is clearly evidenced in [P3]. However, the capacity to achieve it via the recommended £3,600 contribution is undermined by ambiguity over current earnings. The extraction summary flags that 'current employment income appears to be zero' after the client 'quit before he could' enrol in a workplace scheme, yet the report claims in the contributions section that 'current earnings are sufficient to support the recommended contribution level' without stating what those earnings are. [P15] and [P16] confirm uncertainty: adviser notes ask 'When will he stop? Defo 60?' regarding part-time work and note he has 'got DB income, but works part-time'. This is a material inconsistency affecting objective achievability. Objective 4 (consolidate on Aviva Platform for simplification) is not explicitly recorded in the Fact Find objectives section [P3] or in the adviser notes [P15]–[P16]. It appears to be an adviser-proposed solution rather than a stated client objective. Objective 5 (£3,600 gross pension contribution in 2025/26) cannot be reconciled with the employment status evidence. The adviser notes [P15] state 'Possible pension contribution can be done if he's still working – none' and query whether he has a P60/P45. The contribution recommendation rests on unstated or absent current earnings. Objective 6 (do not move Chartered savings) is marked as a client objective in the canonical model but the extraction summary correctly notes it appears in the Fact Find as a stated fact ('Do not wish to move Chartered savings account at this time' is a constraint, not a primary objective). The prioritisation of this as objective 6 is questionable. Guard: Advisers do paraphrase objectives; the substance of objectives 1–3 and 5 are substantively supported by evidence despite wording variation. The guard does not excuse the conditional framing of objective 2 being presented as firm, nor the absence of earnings evidence for objective 5, nor the conflicting risk-level records for objective 1. Guard: Objective 4 (platform consolidation) is not present in the evidence and the recommendation is not shown to bear on an evidenced client need; it remains unsupported. The overall picture is that core objectives around retirement income, risk tolerance and inheritance planning are evidenced but that the supporting detail—employment status, current earnings, actual client commitment to an ISA transfer, and the derivation of the specific contribution amount—contains material gaps and inconsistencies that undermine confidence in the suitability assessment. The fact that the Risk Profile Report records no discussion notes on risk level agreement, combined with conflicting records of whether it is 4 or 5, creates a control gap affecting objective 1. The absence of clear confirmation of current earnings undermines the feasibility of objective 5. The conditional nature of the ISA transfer in evidence versus its presentation as a firm objective in the report is a disclosure gap.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Wants to be fully retired by 60. Min income in retirement is £2,200 net per month. Also looking at investment advice on £60k of his Dad's property money coming in this month, and the £40k cash he currently has so as to avoid paying tax on interest. He has already used his full ISA allowance this year, so has his wife. Potentially interested in transferring his cash ISA to S&S ISA but would have to be really worth his while - not dead set on doing this.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Investment ISA research report 03-12-2025 11-52.md","category":"F","quote":"Risk profile 4... Agreed risk level 5","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"This shows that the recommended Aviva Multi-asset Plus Fund II provides more consistent performance aligned with your Risk Level 4 profile. The fund demonstrates lower volatility than your current mix of Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% and 80% equity funds, which have a combined risk level of 6.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Notes - Barnes.md","category":"C","quote":"Possibly move ISAs – not likely though. Only ATR 4... We need to assess earnings for this year, and pension contributions for this year. Does he have a P60/P45? Poss