ut its use in cashflow is not explained. (7) EXPENDITURE REDUCTION ASSUMPTION: The cashflow modelling [P13] states '0% of any in-year surplus will be saved' but does not explicitly address how the £2,206 expenditure modelling assumption differs from the £3,710 stated actual expenditure on page 2. A reduction of £1,504 monthly (43% decrease) is a material assumption that should be disclosed and justified. (8) GROSS VS NET GUARD: The report mixes gross and net figures without always being clear on basis. The DB pension is stated as £22,000 gross and £1,700 net monthly—this conversion is approximately correct (£22,000÷12=£1,833 gross monthly; net ~93% = £1,700). However, Yvette's £28,000 gross is stated without a specific net equivalent in the report narrative, only estimated. The Fact Find provides no net figure for Yvette's current earnings. (9) STATEMENT ABOUT EARNINGS SUFFICIENCY: The report states 'current earnings are sufficient to support the recommended contribution level' but does not specify what 'current earnings' are after Anthony stopped work. This is either (a) referring only to Yvette's income, in which case it should say so, or (b) referring to income that contradicts the earlier statement that Anthony is no longer working. The ambiguity is a compliance risk. (10) RECOMMENDATIONS RELIANCE: The requirement is about completeness and consistency of income/expenditure data. The recommendation to contribute £3,600 (extracted from context as the advised contribution) depends on affordability derived from surplus income. If surplus is £1,190 monthly (report figure) rather than £383.33 (Fact Find figure), the recommendation's affordability basis is materially different. The report does not reconcile these figures or acknowledge the discrepancy. CONCLUSION: The report contains internal contradictions (two expenditure totals, two surplus figures) and external contradictions with the Fact Find (income total, disposable income, underlying earnings). Some variance is explained by the transition from current (with Anthony earning £890/month driving, which has stopped) to modelled scenarios (post-2026 with Yvette reduced hours, or post-2030 retirement). However, the report does not make these assumptions explicit and does not reconcile its stated current figures with the Fact Find's record. A material portion of the discrepancy remains unexplained.","citations":[{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"You recently retired from a factory role after 35 years of shift work. You did start work part-time driving autistic children to colleges, earning approximately £890 net per month however, you decided that this was not suited to you and have since stopped working altogether. You also receive a defined benefit pension from Aon of £22,000 gross per annum (approximately £1,700 net per month).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"Yvette works full-time as a Medical Case Handler, earning £28,000 gross per annum however, she is intending on reducing her hours to 3 days per week from next year.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"Your current regular expenditure is approximately £2,493 per month covering essential costs such as household bills, transport, and food. You also have discretionary spending of approximately £800 per month on entertainment and leisure, plus around £5,000 annually on holidays. This is covered by your joint expenditure and leaves you with surplus income each month, which you currently save or spend as required.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P22","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"You have confirmed that you retain sufficient liquid capital, either immediately or on short notice, to meet any unexpected emergencies. This provides a reassuring level of financial security as you move forward with your planning. Your cur