{"groupId":"CHK-007","requirement":"[G7.5] Benefit versus cost — fair value. Limb: Consistency. Where costs, charges and the value of recommendations are referenced within the Suitability Report, does the Suitability Report disclose all material charges relevant to the recommendation, including applicable initial, ongoing, product, platform, investment and transaction costs, and where higher costs arise, does the report clearly explain the benefits expected to be received by the client in exchange for those costs and are they supported by and consistent with the evidence provided?","reportSays":"The report asserts 'Lower Costs - Moving to our agency terms will reduce your platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%' [P1, page 14]. It also states 'This small increase in charges is justified by the improved risk alignment and ongoing professional oversight' [P1, page 19]. The report claims 'Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 2.94% per year up to age 60 in order to meet the same value as your current plan. Based on the past performance of your recommended funds, I believe this is achievable given the improved risk alignment and professional oversight' [P1, page 20]. Benefits cited include: Lower Costs (platform charge reduction from 0.32% to 0.21%), Risk Alignment (funds at risk level 6 being moved to risk level 4), Professional Oversight, Adviser Charging Facilitation, Tax-Efficient Contribution (£3,600 gross), and Platform Integration. The report presents these as justifying the switch despite acknowledging a 'small increase in charges'.","fileSays":"Evidence shows existing platform charge of 0.32% [P2, P9] with combined recurring charges of 0.54% including fund charges [internal extraction note]. The recommended Aviva Multi-Asset Plus Fund II has a fund charge of 0.41% (AMC 0.30% + Additional Expenses 0.11%) [P12]. The pension switch research shows 'Impact of Recommended Alternative' as '+0.08%' increase in charges [internal extraction]. The derived figures show: total ongoing charge of £427.00 at 0.54% implies fund value £79,074.07; annual charge difference of £63.00 at 0.08% implies fund value £78,750.00. Existing holdings show past performance: Vanguard LifeStrategy 60% Equity (3M: 6.03%, 6M: 13.74%, 1Y: 13.86%, 3Y: 35.92%, 5Y: 42.45%) and Vanguard LifeStrategy 80% Equity (3M: 7.36%, 6M: 17.61%, 1Y: 17.03%, 3Y: 45.41%, 5Y: 66.42%) [P2, P9]. Recommended fund performance: Aviva Multi-Asset Plus Fund II (3M: 5.58%, 6M: 12.61%, 1Y: 11.92%, 3Y: 33.59%, 5Y: 36.21%) [P8, P11, P12]. The existing arrangement shows combined discrete performance of 12.86% (2021), -9.65% (2022), 11.23% (2023), 11.95% (2024), 13.47% (YTD) [P5]. Risk level of existing portfolio (weighted average of 60%/80% LifeStrategy split at 35.02%/64.95%) approximates risk 6; recommended fund at risk level 4 [P8].","discrepancies":["Report claims 'Lower Costs' by reducing platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%, but total recurring charges increase by 0.08% (from 0.54% to 0.62% or higher when adviser fees added)","Report asserts required growth rate of 2.94% 'is achievable' based on past performance, but provides no explicit forward-looking fund performance projection or analysis; past performance data shows existing funds outperformed recommended fund over 5 years (existing combined ~58% vs recommended ~36.21%)","Report states benefits include Professional Oversight and Adviser Charging Facilitation but does not quantify the cost of 1% ongoing adviser fee in the benefit-cost analysis section","Report does not provide aggregated all-in cost illustration showing combined platform charge, fund charge, and adviser fee impact in a single figure for easy comparison","Benefits (risk alignment, professional oversight, tax efficiency, platform integration) are stated but not weighted or quantified against the cost increase; the cost justification relies on qualitative assertions rather than numerical analysis","Report acknowledges 'small increase in charges' but does not specify the magnitude (£63 per annum at £78,750 fund value = 0.08%) in the suitability section where costs are discussed"],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report presents a contradiction on its face. It opens with 'Lower Costs' as a headline benefit, citing platform charge reduction from 0.32% to 0.21% (0.11 percentage point saving). However, the underlying evidence and derived figures show total recurring charges increase by 0.08 percentage points (£63 annually on a £78,750 fund value). This discrepancy arises because the platform saving (0.11pp) is outweighed by the fund charge difference and, critically, the addition of a 1% ongoing adviser fee is not transparently netted into the headline benefit. The report acknowledges a 'small increase in charges' later [P1, page 19] but does not reconcile this with the 'Lower Costs' claim on page 14. The internal extraction flagged this inconsistency: existing platform 0.32% vs new platform 0.21% (stated saving of 0.11%), but the switch charges table shows total recurring charge impact of +0.08%, indicating the fund cost is higher. On the Risk Alignment benefit: the report correctly identifies the existing portfolio (Vanguard LifeStrategy 60%/80% split) as risk 6 and the client profile as risk 4, and the recommended fund MAP II is correctly stated as risk 4. This benefit is genuine and supported. Performance: the report states the required growth rate of 2.94% 'is achievable given the improved risk alignment and professional oversight' [P1, page 20]. This is presented as justification for accepting higher costs, but it is an assertion without quantitative support. Past performance data shows existing funds materially outperformed the recommended fund over multiple periods (e.g., 5-year: existing ~58% cumulative vs recommended 36.21%), which contradicts the implicit claim that the recommended fund's performance will be adequate to offset higher charges. A client requiring 2.94% growth to break even faces higher hurdle than the existing portfolio's historical performance difference. The Professional Oversight and Adviser Charging Facilitation benefits are stated as client-specific but are not costed or quantified. The 1% ongoing adviser fee is mentioned in the assumptions but not integrated into the benefit-cost narrative in the main suitability findings section. Platform Integration is stated as a benefit of keeping pension, ISA and GIA on same platform, but this is generic, not client-specific, and no evidence is provided that consolidation itself generates sufficient value to justify switching costs or higher platform fees. Tax-Efficient Contribution (£3,600 gross with 20% relief) is legitimate but unrelated to the fund switch decision and thus not a defence of the higher costs. Overall, the report discloses multiple benefits and acknowledges a cost increase but does not aggregate costs or weight benefits quantitatively. The 'Lower Costs' headline contradicts the net cost impact, creating a material inconsistency. The justification for accepting higher costs rests on qualitative assertions (risk alignment is sound, professional oversight is valuable, performance is achievable) rather than on numerical comparison. The basis for claiming 2.94% growth is 'achievable' is not established; past performance of the recommended fund does not support this claim as strongly as the report implies.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Lower Costs - Moving to our agency terms will reduce your platform charge from 0.32% to 0.21%.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"This small increase in charges is justified by the improved risk alignment and ongoing professional oversight.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report - Barnes