tions 2, 3, 4, 5 despite being stated as considered","Numerical comparison between recommended solution total charges and stakeholder pension charges to support 'more cost effective' claim","Explanation for why maturity values show no material difference (£0-£100 across growth scenarios) yet switching is recommended","Analysis of whether risk alignment benefit (moving from 6.6 to 4) justifies zero/minimal maturity value benefit","Comparison of performance between existing Vanguard funds and recommended Aviva Multi-asset Plus II beyond the 5-year period cited for ISA alternative","Safeguarded benefits analysis confirming 'no benefits were listed' is accurate and complete for the existing arrangement"],"analysis":"The requirement asks whether 'other alternatives provide better outcomes for the client.' The analysis must distinguish between: (1) alternatives the report claims to have considered, (2) whether those alternatives were actually compared, and (3) whether the recommended solution represents a better outcome. Report-Side: The report asserts five pension alternatives and three product-level alternatives (Stakeholder, Investment pathway, ISA) were considered. Only one—the 'do nothing' option—receives substantive rejection reasoning (risk misalignment). Options 2-5 are listed as considered but provide no rejection reasons whatsoever. Additional alternatives (Stakeholder, Investment pathway, Moneybox ISA) receive stated reasons. Evidence-Side: The pension switch report provides detailed maturity value projections showing no material benefit from switching (£0 at low/mid growth, £100 at high growth when both grow at same rate). The reduction in yield (charges impact) is essentially identical (0.6-0.7%) for both arrangements. The critical yield required is 0.0-5.9% depending on scenario. Discrepancy 1 – Risk weighting: The report states current funds have 'risk level of 6' but the existing holdings show Vanguard 60% (risk 5, 35.02%) and Vanguard 80% (risk 7, 64.95%)