{"groupId":"G10.4","requirement":"Vulnerability has a material impact on the recommendation (overlay). Where the Suitability Report includes information relating to client vulnerabilities, support needs, communication preferences, capacity, comprehension requirements or duty of care considerations, is this information fully supported by the evidence provided?","reportSays":"The report asserts one impact of vulnerability on the recommendation: 'Limited investment knowledge supported by recommendation for professionally managed multi-asset fund rather than requiring active fund selection' [P24]. The report identifies the client as having 'very little understanding or knowledge when it comes to investing' [P16], describes the agreed risk profile as 'Cautious Balanced' appropriate for those with 'limited or moderate experience of investments and do not find financial matters particularly easy to understand' [P7, P15], and states 'we have recommended a multi-asset fund because it provides a professionally managed, diversified investment solution' [P24]. Capacity for loss is assessed as 'Medium-High' based on 'good level of guaranteed income from State and Private Pensions' and 'significant funds to cover' shortfalls [P16]. The report makes no reference to foreseeable harms, support needs beyond fund selection, communication adjustments, third-party involvement, or enhanced duty of care measures.","fileSays":"The file contains extensive evidence of vulnerability indicators and circumstances requiring support: (1) Knowledge/experience: Client responses show 'No understanding / knowledge' of stocks and shares and 'Very little understanding / knowledge' of pension planning [P2]; (2) Psychometric assessment: Risk profile identified as 'Cautious Balanced' for those who 'may take some time to make investment decisions and tend to be somewhat anxious about those decisions' and 'do not find financial matters particularly easy to understand' [P7, P15]; (3) Capacity for loss: The risk profile report states 'No additional notes were recorded' in both the 'Capacity for loss questionnaire' section [P1] and 'Agreed risk level' section [P12], indicating assessment conducted but no specific support needs documented; (4) Communications: Fact Find shows 'No Requirement' for 'Accessible format requirement' and 'No Preference' for 'Preferred delivery method', but records that 'Client did not have time to complete an ATRQ today, so have sent him an email version to complete himself' [P14]; (5) No evidence of Power of Attorney, trusted contacts, family involvement, or enhanced communication protocols; (6) Affordability and life circumstances: Client is transitioning from 35 years in factory work to part-time driving (now ceased), creating employment income uncertainty [P3, P4]; client retired but with DB income and uncertain part-time work end date [P4]; (7) Cashflow resilience: Analysis shows narrow margins with 'Total Monthly Disposable Income' of £383.33 against £2,206.67 expenditure [P13], and multiple stress scenarios modelled (Market Crash, Tony Predeceases, Yvette Predeceases, Higher Inflation) [P8, P9, P10, P17].","discrepancies":["Report identifies limited investment knowledge as driver for multi-asset fund recommendation but does not reference the client's 'No understanding / knowledge' responses on stocks/shares or 'Very little understanding' on pensions from the investment questionnaire [P2].","Report describes risk profile as 'Cautious Balanced' but does not quote or reference the full descriptor that those with such profiles 'do not find financial matters particularly easy to understand' [P7, P15], which is a material FG21/1 driver.","Report asserts 'Medium-High' capacity for loss but provides no evidence of how the capacity for loss questionnaire was completed or what notes were recorded, despite the risk profile report showing 'No additional notes were recorded' in that section [P1, P12].","Report makes no reference to the narrow disposable income margin (£383.33 monthly) [P13] despite recommending an additional £3,600 annual contribution, nor addresses how this affects vulnerability to market volatility.","Report does not reference or address the employment income uncertainty (retirement after 35 years, subsequent part-time work now ceased) [P3, P4] in relation to ability to sustain recommended contributions or resilience to investment losses.","Report identifies support need (professionally managed fund instead of active selection) but does not document other potential support requirements around communication, monitoring, or decision review given the limited financial knowledge and anxiety indicators.","Report contains no discussion of foreseeable harms, no mention of review triggers, no reference to enhanced monitoring given the Cautious Balanced profile indicators of anxiety about investment decisions."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report supports its core recommendation (multi-asset fund for limited knowledge) with evidence that the client demonstrates very limited investment knowledge and anxiety about financial decisions. However, the overlay requirement asks whether vulnerability information is 'fully supported' and whether the recommendation 'accounts for' identified characteristics across support needs, communication, and duty of care considerations. Three material gaps exist: (1) Incompleteness of vulnerability assessment documentation: The risk profile questionnaire responses [P2] show clear FG21/1 drivers (low capability, anxiety indicators), and the risk profile descriptor [P7, P15] explicitly names 'limited experience' and difficulty understanding financial matters as the profile rationale, yet the report does not integrate this language into its reasoning about why multi-asset funds were chosen. The capacity for loss section shows 'No additional notes were recorded' [P1, P12], suggesting the assessment was conducted but not documented beyond the concluded 'Medium-High' rating. (2) Narrow consideration of support needs: The report addresses only fund selection simplicity but does not evidence consideration of other support dimensions—communication accessibility (though 'No Requirement' is recorded [P14]), decision-making support, or review protocols. For a client described as having 'very little understanding' and a profile indicating they 'tend to be somewhat anxious', silence on these dimensions is material. (3) Failure to address material circumstantial vulnerability: The client's employment history shows a 35-year factory career followed by brief part-time work now ceased, leaving employment income status ambiguous [P3, P4]. Combined with monthly disposable income of only £383.33 [P13] and a recommendation to contribute £3,600 annually, the resilience to recommended contributions and investment losses is not evidenced. Multiple stress scenarios are present in the file [P8, P9, P10, P17] but the report does not reference capacity to sustain the plan under adverse conditions given the limited knowledge and narrow income margins. The guards instruct that age alone is not vulnerability and that genuine absence of assessment should be noted rather than manufactured. Here, assessment has occurred (questionnaires completed, risk profile assigned, capacity determined), but the report's treatment of vulnerability in its recommendation is narrow—it justifies one feature (fund simplicity) while leaving other dimensions undocumented. This is not absence of assessment; it is incomplete integration of assessment findings into the recommendation rationale and support design. Under FG21/1 and PRIN 2A.2.1R, where a client presents capability constraints and anxiety indicators, the duty of care requires that the recommendation demonstrate how the firm will support informed decision-making and manage foreseeable harm—not just select an easier product.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"[D] Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":"Do you understand how stocks and shares work and do you know the risks associated with them? No understanding / knowledge. Do you understand how pension planning and income in retirement works and do you know the risks associated with them? Very little understanding / knowledge.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"[D] Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":"If you have limited or moderate experience of investments and do not find financial matters particularly easy to understand then you may be suited to a Cautious Balanced approach to investing. You may also be suited to this approach if you take some time to make investment decisions and tend to be somewhat anxious about those decisions.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"[D] Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":"As a Cautious Balanced investor you tend to see yourself as quite a cautious person and are inclined to view risk negatively rather than as a source of opportunity. If you have limited or moderate experience of investments and do not find financial matters particularly easy to understand then you may be suited to a Cautious Balanced approach to investing. You may also be suited to this approach if you take some time to make investment decisions and tend to be somewhat anxious about those decisions.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"[I] Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on your investor experience questionnaire completed on 9 August 2025, you describe yourself as having very little understanding or knowledge when it comes to investing.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"[D] Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":"Capacity for loss questionnaire. No additional notes were recorded.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"[D] Risk profile report 11 08 2025 08 58.md","category":"D","quote":"Agreed risk level. This section is a summary of how the risk level was agreed. These details were recorded following the discussion: No additional notes were recorded.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"[B] Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Total Net Monthly Income £2,590.00. Total Monthly Expenditure £2,206.67. Total Monthly Disposable Income £383.33","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"[C] Notes - Barnes.md","category":"C","quote":"About to receive circa £100k but will use £40k on kitchen/bathroom. Has another £40k in high interest savings, but only look at investing the £60k. We need to assess earnings for this year, and pension contributions for this year. Does he have a P60/P45? Possible pension contribution can be done if he's still working. – none. Aviva appears to be only pension and no contributions made.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"[C] Notes - Barnes.md","category":"C","quote":"Retired, gets DB income, but works part-time. When will he stop? Defo 60?","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"[B] Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"Client did not have time to complete an ATRQ today, so have sent him an email version to complete himself on Defaqto. Accessible format requirement: No Requirement. Preferred delivery method: No Preference.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"[I] Suitability Report - Barnes.md","category":"I","quote":"We have recommended a multi-asset fund because it provides a professionally managed, diversified investment solution that aligns with your financial goals and risk profile.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Weak justification","Disclosure shortfall","Compliance concern"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

